---
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import ExtensionStore from "../components/ExtensionStore.astro";
import FeatureDemo from "../components/FeatureDemo.astro";
import PageSection from "../components/PageSection.astro";
import ReleaseLink from "../components/ReleaseLink.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { copy } from "../lib/i18n";

const lang = "en";
const text = copy[lang];

const schema = [
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#website",
    "@type": "WebSite",
    alternateName: ["Lilo", "getlilo.app"],
    inLanguage: ["en", "fr"],
    name: "Lilo Browser",
    publisher: {
      "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#author",
    },
    url: "https://getlilo.app/",
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#author",
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Alexis Rouillard",
    url: "https://github.com/Alexays",
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#app",
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    alternateName: ["Lilo Browser", "Lilo for Mac"],
    applicationCategory: "BrowserApplication",
    applicationSubCategory: "Web browser",
    author: {
      "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#author",
    },
    description:
      "Lilo Browser is a free, open-source, private by default Arc alternative for Mac that is not Chromium: native WebKit that runs Chrome extensions, with terminal tabs and an MCP server for AI agents.",
    disambiguatingDescription:
      "Lilo Browser is a web browser app for macOS, not the Lilo search engine at lilo.org.",
    downloadUrl:
      "https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/releases/latest/download/Lilo.dmg",
    image: "https://getlilo.app/og.png",
    inLanguage: "en",
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    license: "https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT",
    name: "Lilo",
    offers: {
      "@type": "Offer",
      price: "0",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
    },
    operatingSystem: "macOS 26+",
    publisher: {
      "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#author",
    },
    releaseNotes: "https://getlilo.app/changelog/",
    sameAs: ["https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo"],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/",
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo Browser: a WebKit browser for Mac with Spaces and Chrome extensions"
  description="Lilo Browser is a free, open-source, private by default Arc alternative for Mac that is not Chromium: native WebKit that runs Chrome extensions, with terminal tabs and an MCP server for AI agents."
  schema={schema}
  hero={{
    heading: text.homeHeading,
    description: text.homeDescription,
    tag: text.homeBeta,
  }}
>
  <ReleaseLink lang={lang} slot="announcement" />
  <Demo lang={lang} slot="preview" />

  <PageSection id="native" layout="native">
    <div>
      <div class="head" style="margin-bottom:32px">
        <span class="tag">Made for the Mac</span>
        <h2>Built on WebKit. Not on a second Chrome.</h2>
        <p>
          Lilo uses the engine already inside macOS, written in Swift. No
          Chromium bundled, no Electron, and your Chrome extensions still
          install from the Chrome Web Store. Your Mac spends its energy on your
          pages.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="claims">
        <div class="claim">
          <span class="ic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              width="22"
              height="22"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
              <path d="m15.5 8.5-2 5-5 2 2-5z" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3>The engine Safari uses</h3>
            <p>
              Apple tunes WebKit for Apple silicon, video decoding and
              sandboxing. Lilo gets every macOS update for free.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="claim">
          <span class="ic" style="background:#f4bb8c">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              width="22"
              height="22"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <rect x="2.5" y="7" width="17" height="10" rx="2.5" />
              <path d="M21.5 10.5v3" />
              <path d="m11.5 9-2 3h3l-2 3" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3>Easy on the battery</h3>
            <p>
              Idle tabs freeze, then sleep. Background Spaces stop working when
              you are not looking at them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="claim">
          <span class="ic" style="background:#92a8ff">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              width="22"
              height="22"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <rect x="6" y="6" width="12" height="12" rx="2" />
              <rect x="9.5" y="9.5" width="5" height="5" rx="1" />
              <path d="M9 3v3M15 3v3M9 18v3M15 18v3M3 9h3M3 15h3M18 9h3M18 15h3" />
            </svg>
          </span>
          <div>
            <h3>Light on memory</h3>
            <p>
              Tab memory follows how much RAM your Mac really has, and gives it
              back under pressure.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="bench" id="bench">
      <h3>Energy, measured</h3>
      <p>What the Mac draws for five busy tabs, above an idle Mac.</p>
      <ol class="bars" aria-label="Power above an idle Mac, per browser">
        <li class="lilo">
          <span>Lilo</span>
          <span class="bar" style="--v:10.0%"></span>
          <span class="val">37 mW</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Safari</span>
          <span class="bar" style="--v:9.2%"></span>
          <span class="val">34 mW</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Chrome</span>
          <span class="bar" style="--v:27.6%"></span>
          <span class="val">102 mW</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Firefox</span>
          <span class="bar" style="--v:100.0%"></span>
          <span class="val">370 mW</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="note">
        Idle, Lilo and Safari stay within the noise of an idle Mac, Chrome adds
        62 mW and Firefox 45 mW. MacBook Pro M4 Pro, macOS 27, medians of 3
        runs.
        <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/docs/ENERGY.md">
          Method and full results
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="extensions" layout="ext">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Chrome extensions</span>
      <h2>The Chrome Web Store, with an "Add to Lilo" button.</h2>
      <p>
        Browse the store you know and install in one click. Lilo runs Manifest
        V3 extensions on WebKit, keeps them updated, and gives each profile its
        own set.
      </p>
      <div class="cats">
        <span>Password managers</span>
        <span>Ad blockers</span>
        <span>Dark mode</span>
        <span>Dev tools</span>
        <span>Writing helpers</span>
        <span>Price trackers</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ExtensionStore lang={lang} />
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="privacy" layout="privacy">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Private by design</span>
      <h2>What you do in Lilo stays on your Mac.</h2>
      <p>
        No account, no telemetry, no analytics. The project runs no server, so
        there is nowhere for your browsing to go. And every line is open for you
        to check.
      </p>
      <p class="opt">
        On by default: Global Privacy Control (macOS 27). One switch away in
        Settings: HTTPS-Only mode.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shields">
      <div class="shield">
        <span class="sic" style="background:#9bd3b4">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M12 3 5 6v5c0 4.5 3 8.3 7 10 4-1.7 7-5.5 7-10V6z" />
            <path d="m9 12 2 2 4-4" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Trackers blocked out of the box</h3>
          <p>
            Ads, trackers and cookie banners are filtered with community lists
            kept up to date, and sites embedded in others can't follow you from
            one to the next. Nothing to install.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shield">
        <span class="sic" style="background:#92a8ff">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <rect x="3" y="4" width="8" height="16" rx="2" />
            <rect x="13" y="4" width="8" height="16" rx="2" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Sign-ins kept apart</h3>
          <p>
            Each Space can have its own profile, with its own cookies and
            storage. Private Spaces forget everything when they close.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shield">
        <span class="sic" style="background:#f4bb8c">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <rect x="5" y="10" width="14" height="10" rx="2" />
            <path d="M8 10V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Agents on a short leash</h3>
          <p>
            Agents never see your private tabs, and Lilo asks you before one
            sends, pays, deletes or publishes anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shield">
        <span class="sic" style="background:#c3a5ea">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
            <path d="M3 12h18M12 3c2.5 2.7 2.5 15.3 0 18M12 3c-2.5 2.7-2.5 15.3 0 18" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Apple's engine and sandbox</h3>
          <p>
            Pages run in WebKit's sandboxed processes and get Apple's security
            fixes with every macOS update.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="shield">
        <span class="sic" style="background:#f5d77a">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            width="22"
            height="22"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M8 6h12M8 12h12M8 18h12" />
            <path d="M4 6h.01M4 12h.01M4 18h.01" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3>Every request, listed</h3>
          <p>
            Besides the pages you open, Lilo talks to a handful of services
            (filter lists, updates). Each one is
            <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/PRIVACY.md#requests-lilo-makes-on-its-own">
              listed, with how to turn it off
            </a>
            .
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="move">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Built for how you browse</span>
      <h2>Everything in its Space.</h2>
      <p>
        The sidebar you know, with the details that make it yours. Move in from
        Safari, Chrome, Brave, Edge, Vivaldi, Opera, Arc, Dia, Comet, Chromium,
        Firefox or Zen in five minutes, straight from your Mac.{" "}
        <a href="/moving/">How moving works</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="move">
      <div class="mv" style="background:#b39df4">
        <h3>Spaces with their own sign-ins</h3>
        <p>
          Work and Perso side by side in one window, each with its cookies and
          extensions. Swipe between them.
        </p>
        <div class="keys">
          <kbd>⌃1</kbd>
          <kbd>⌃2</kbd>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#92a8ff">
        <h3>Tabs on any edge</h3>
        <p>
          Left like Arc, right to keep the page first, or one strip on top or
          bottom like Safari. Drag the sidebar to another edge to move it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#9ac7eb">
        <h3>Peek</h3>
        <p>
          Links from your pinned tabs open over the page. Keep reading, expand
          it to a tab, or put it in a split.
        </p>
        <div class="keys">
          <kbd>⇧ click</kbd>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#f0a8c8">
        <h3>Little window</h3>
        <p>
          A quick window from any app, for one link or one search. Send it to a
          Space when it turns into something.
        </p>
        <div class="keys">
          <kbd>⌘⌥N</kbd>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#9bd3b4">
        <h3>Tabs that tidy themselves</h3>
        <p>
          Unpinned tabs you stopped using are archived after 12 hours, or when
          you choose. Find them again in a keystroke.
        </p>
        <div class="keys">
          <kbd>⌘⇧K</kbd>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#f4bb8c">
        <h3>Command bar</h3>
        <p>
          Tabs, history, bookmarks and every action in one field, ranked by what
          you use. Open a result in a split or a Peek.
        </p>
        <div class="keys">
          <kbd>⌘T</kbd>
          <kbd>⌥↩</kbd>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#e8d98f">
        <h3>Your shortcuts</h3>
        <p>
          Every shortcut can be remapped. Start from a preset, conflicts are
          flagged as you type.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#8fd5cf">
        <h3>Links land in the right Space</h3>
        <p>
          Links opened from Mail or Slack follow your rules: this domain to
          Work, the rest to the Little window.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mv" style="background:#c3a5ea">
        <h3>Split view</h3>
        <p>
          Up to four tabs side by side, a terminal next to your docs, restored
          exactly as you left them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="build" layout="build">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">For people who build</span>
      <h2>Your terminal lives in the sidebar.</h2>
      <p>
        Terminal tabs sit next to your docs and your localhost, on a Metal
        renderer built on libghostty. Split one beside a page, and the sidebar
        shows its branch and pending changes at a glance.
      </p>
      <div class="feat-list">
        <div>
          <b>Stays awake while it works.</b> A long build or an agent run keeps
          your Mac awake, even with the lid closed, then lets it sleep when the
          command ends.
        </div>
        <div>
          <b>Tells you when it's done.</b> A finished or waiting command lights
          up its Space, so you can browse while it runs.
        </div>
        <div>
          <b>Review before you commit.</b> The changes view shows every diff of
          the repo, images included.
        </div>
        <div>
          <b>Talks to your tools.</b> AppleScript, Shortcuts and Focus filters,
          plus Web Serial for boards like Arduino and ESP.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <FeatureDemo lang={lang} mode="terminal" />
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="agents" layout="agents">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">For AI agents</span>
      <h2>Your agents get their own Space.</h2>
      <p>
        Lilo has a built-in MCP server. Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or any MCP
        client open tabs, click, type, and read pages, the console and the
        network, in a real browser beside you.
      </p>
      <div class="ag-list">
        <div>
          <span>
            <b>Their own Space.</b> Agents open tabs in a folder of the Agents
            Space, signed in nowhere by default. Your keyboard, your mouse and
            your tabs stay yours.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>
            <b>You say yes first.</b> Lilo asks before an agent sends, pays,
            deletes or publishes. Each agent is approved once, and none ever
            sees a private Space.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>
            <b>One click to connect.</b> In Settings, Connect adds Lilo to
            Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex or Cursor. Any other client
            copies the configuration.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ag-cta">
        <a class="pill dark" href="/ai-agents/">
          How agents work
        </a>
        <span>No AI inside Lilo: bring the agent you already use.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <FeatureDemo lang={lang} mode="mcp" />
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="faq" layout="faq">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Questions</span>
      <h2>Good to know.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Is Lilo free?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Lilo is free and open source under the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/LICENSE">
            MIT license
          </a>
          . There is no paid tier and no account to create.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who makes Lilo, and will it last?</summary>
        <p>
          Alexays, who has also maintained the open-source Waybar since 2018.
          The part that keeps you safe is not his alone: WebKit ships with
          macOS, so Apple's security fixes reach Lilo with every macOS update.
          The code is MIT licensed, so it stays yours to run and fork whatever
          happens, and no company can switch it off. What comes next is in the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/ROADMAP.md">
            roadmap
          </a>
          , and you can back the work on{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/Alexays">GitHub Sponsors</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I put my tabs on top, like Safari or Chrome?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Tabs go on any edge of the window: left, right, top or bottom.
          Spaces, pinned tabs and folders come along. Switch anytime in
          Settings, from the command bar, or by dragging the sidebar to another
          edge.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is Lilo built on?</summary>
        <p>
          WebKit, the engine Safari uses, in an app written in Swift and AppKit,
          with its sidebar and settings in Svelte. No Chromium is bundled. On
          top of it, Lilo runs Chrome extensions from the Chrome Web Store.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which extensions work?</summary>
        <p>
          Manifest V3 extensions from the Chrome Web Store install with one
          click and keep updating. Manifest V2 extensions and blocking{" "}
          <code>webRequest</code> are not supported, and a few extensions rely
          on other features only Chrome has. The{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/COMPATIBILITY.md">
            compatibility notes
          </a>{" "}
          list exactly what differs.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>
          Do Netflix, Spotify and other streaming services work?
        </summary>
        <p>
          Lilo plays protected video and music through Apple's FairPlay, the
          same system Safari uses, so services that support Safari, like
          Netflix, Prime Video, Disney+, Apple TV+ and Spotify, should play as
          they do there. YouTube streams in 4K HDR. Widevine is not available in
          WebKit, so a service that requires it plays at lower quality or not at
          all, and offline downloads in the browser are not supported.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I bring my stuff from another browser?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Lilo imports bookmarks, history and sign-ins from Safari, Chrome,
          Brave, Edge, Vivaldi, Opera, Arc, Dia, Comet, Chromium, Firefox and
          Zen, open tabs where the browser keeps them, and offers to reinstall
          your Chrome Web Store extensions.{" "}
          <a href="/moving/">See what comes with you</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What about my passwords?</summary>
        <p>
          Lilo keeps no passwords itself and never reads them from other
          browsers. Setup offers the manager you already use, such as NordPass,
          Bitwarden, 1Password, Proton Pass, Dashlane or KeePassXC. iCloud
          Passwords comes as soon as Apple approves it.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is there sync, or a mobile app?</summary>
        <p>
          No, by design: Lilo has no account and no server, so there is nothing
          to sync through. It is a Mac app only. Your password manager keeps
          syncing your passwords everywhere, and importing changes nothing in
          your other browser, so its own sync to your phone keeps working.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does Lilo have AI built in?</summary>
        <p>
          No. If you turn it on, the agents you already use, like Claude Code,
          Codex or Cursor, can drive Lilo over a local MCP server, in their own
          Space. Each agent is approved once, never sees your private Spaces,
          and Lilo asks you before it sends, pays or deletes anything.{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/docs/AI-AGENTS.md">
            How agents work
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where is my data?</summary>
        <p>
          On your Mac, kept apart per profile. No account, no telemetry, and the
          project runs no server, so nothing you do is sent to us.{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/PRIVACY.md">
            Read the privacy notes
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is Lilo related to the Lilo search engine?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Lilo Browser is an independent, open-source web browser for Mac.
          The Lilo search engine at lilo.org is a separate project with no link
          to it. In Lilo, you pick your search engine in Settings.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why only on Mac?</summary>
        <p>
          Lilo is built on the WebKit engine that ships with macOS and written
          in Swift. That is what keeps it light and easy on the battery. It
          needs macOS 26 or later.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How can I help?</summary>
        <p>
          Star the repo, report what breaks, send a pull request, or back the
          work on{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/Alexays">GitHub Sponsors</a>. The
          whole interface is a Svelte layer you can change and rebuild in
          seconds.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </PageSection>
</Layout>
